        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWS signature verification does not bind the declared `alg` header (fully attacker-controlled, taken from the untrusted token) to the actual type/intent of the key material supplied by the caller. jws.py:_sig_matches_keys calls jwk.construct(key, alg) for any `key` that is not already a jose Key instance, and jwk.construct (jose/jwk.py lines 63-79) picks the Key class purely from the `alg` string via get_key(), with no check that raw key bytes/PEM/dict actually correspond to that algorithm family. Combined with _verify_signature only enforcing an algorithm whitelist when the caller explicitly supplies `algorithms` (`algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms` at line 258 \u2014 skipped entirely when algorithms is None/omitted), this is the classic JWT 'algorithm confusion' gap: a caller who stores an RSA/EC public key as raw PEM/dict and does not pass a restrictive `algorithms` allow-list can have an attacker-forged HS256 token (using the public PEM bytes as the HMAC secret) accepted as valid.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read jose/jws.py and jose/jwk.py directly and reproduced the analyst's mechanism exactly: `_verify_signature` (jws.py:253-268) only rejects a disallowed `alg` when the caller explicitly passes `algorithms` (\"algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms\" at line 258) \u2014 if `algorithms` is None/omitted (which is the default for both `jws.verify`'s signature and `jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=None, ...)` in jwt.py:59/145), that check is skipped entirely. It then calls `_sig_matches_keys(keys, signing_input, signature, alg)` (line 263) with `alg` taken verbatim, attacker-controlled, from the unverified header (line 254: `alg = header.get(\"alg\")`). `_sig_matches_keys` (jws.py:209-218) does `jwk.construct(key, alg)` (line 212) for any key that isn't already a `Key` instance, and `jwk.construct` (jwk.py:63-79) picks the Key subclass purely via `get_key(algorithm)` (line 76) and instantiates it (line 79) with no check that the supplied key material actually matches that algorithm family \u2014 i.e. nothing binds the header's claimed `alg` to the real type of the caller-supplied key. This is a verified textbook JWT \"algorithm confusion\" design gap, not merely a theoretical reading of the code: a confirmed scan_id-scoped Cypher query shows every line in this chain (254-264 in `_verify_signature`, 209-218 in `_sig_matches_keys`) has `reachable_from_entry=true`, `hop_distance=1`. I also checked for mitigations the analyst didn't mention: `HMACKey.__init__` in both backends (native.py:39-50, cryptography_backend.py:558-569) does block raw RSA-PEM-public/x509-cert/\"ssh-rsa\" strings from being used as an HMAC secret, and dict-shaped JWKs are blocked unless `kty==\"oct\"` \u2014 so the classic \"feed the RSA PEM public key in as the HS256 secret\" case is explicitly guarded in this codebase. However that blocklist is incomplete: it does not cover OpenSSH-wire-format EC public keys (e.g. \"ecdsa-sha2-nistp256 AAAA...\") or ssh-ed25519 strings, nor raw DER bytes \u2014 so the confusion attack is still live whenever a caller's key is in one of those uncovered forms and `algorithms` is omitted. This matches the installed version (jose/__init__.py: `__version__ = \"3.3.0\"`), which is the exact version range affected by the published python-jose algorithm-confusion advisory for this gap. Net: the design flaw is real, confirmed in source and graph, and remains exploitable in concrete, non-hypothetical scenarios \u2014 not fully closed by the existing partial guard."
          },
          "locations": [
