        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The same missing algorithm/key-type binding exists on the JWE decryption path: jose/jwe.py decrypt() builds the content-encryption/key-wrap key via `jwk.construct(key, alg)` at line 108 using the attacker-controlled `alg` header value from the untrusted JWE token, with no check that the caller-supplied `key` object was intended for that algorithm (e.g. an attacker could flip the header's alg from an asymmetric key-wrap algorithm to 'dir' to force the server's key bytes to be reinterpreted directly as the content-encryption key).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against jose/jwe.py source (not just graph claims). decrypt() reads `alg = header[\"alg\"]` (line 97) straight from the attacker-supplied/untrusted JWE header, validates it only against the library-wide `ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED` set (line 99) \u2014 not against any caller-specified allowlist or any property of the `key` object the caller passed in \u2014 and then immediately does `key = jwk.construct(key, alg)` (line 108), letting the untrusted `alg` value pick which Key subclass (DIRKey/AESKey/RSAKey/HMACKey/...) wraps the caller's raw key material. If `alg == ALGORITHMS.DIR` (line 127), the code skips any unwrap step and uses `_get_key_bytes_from_key(key)` (line 138) to feed the caller's raw key bytes directly in as the content-encryption key \u2014 confirmed in backends/base.py's DIRKey, whose constructor just does `self._key = ensure_binary(key_data)` with no type/algorithm compatibility check at all. This is a real, unmitigated gap: I compared it to jose/jws.py in the same codebase, where the equivalent signature-verification path (`jws.verify`/`jws._verify_signature`, lines 52-77/253-259) takes a mandatory `algorithms` allowlist and rejects any `alg` not in it \u2014 the documented fix pattern for the library's own JWS algorithm-confusion history (CVE-2024-33663-class issue). `jwe.decrypt` has no such allowlist param and `jwt.py` only wires the allowlist through to `jws.verify`, never to `jwe`. Graph query confirms every token on this path (lines 97, 99, 108, 127) is `reachable_from_entry=true` with `jwe.decrypt` itself registered as a reachable EntryPoint handler. Exploitability still depends on how an embedding application uses jwe.decrypt (e.g., reusing one key across algorithms, or passing asymmetric key material that could coerce into a usable symmetric CEK) \u2014 the analyst's own LOW confidence and my inability to prove a concrete forged-plaintext/confidentiality-bypass chain without a specific calling application reflects that conditionality \u2014 but the structural defect (attacker-controlled alg header drives key-type reinterpretation with zero binding check) is real, present, and reachable, so I confirm the finding at reduced severity rather than reject or stay inconclusive."
          },
          "locations": [
