        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-208",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "jose/jwt.py:_validate_at_hash compares the token's `at_hash` claim against the locally computed expected hash with a plain `!=` rather than a constant-time comparison, a smaller-scale instance of the same missing-timing-safe-comparison pattern found in the JWE auth-tag check.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against source: jose/jwt.py's _validate_at_hash computes expected_hash = calculate_at_hash(access_token, ALGORITHMS.HASHES[algorithm]) (line 452) and then checks `if claims[\"at_hash\"] != expected_hash:` (line 457), raising JWTClaimsError at line 458. Both expected_hash and claims[\"at_hash\"] are plain Python str objects, and `!=` on str performs a short-circuiting, length/prefix-dependent comparison -- not constant-time. This is a real inconsistency with the rest of the codebase: jose/backends/native.py:69 correctly uses `hmac.compare_digest(sig, self.sign(msg))` for HMAC signature verification, showing the project knows to use constant-time comparison for security-sensitive equality checks but did not apply it here. Graph confirms the call `claims[\"at_hash\"] != expected_hash` (uid 331d2690a550822fa95a1ca02d8b6b7638d708b6) has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, and is reached from EntryPoint jose/jwt.py:<module>.decode (decode() calls jws.verify() then _validate_claims() -> _validate_at_hash() when verify_at_hash is set and access_token is supplied). However, exploitability is limited: decode() verifies the JWS signature (line 145) before this check is ever reached, so an attacker without the signing key cannot cheaply generate the many distinct validly-signed tokens needed to probe the timing oracle byte-by-byte, and expected_hash is a one-way SHA-2 hash (not a MAC/secret), so even a perfect timing oracle wouldn't let an attacker forge a matching access_token without a hash preimage. This is a genuine but minor info-leak pattern (CWE-208), matching the analyst's own LOW-confidence rating; I'm confirming the code pattern exists exactly as cited, with low practical severity."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "jose/jwt.py",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
