        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-345",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "No code anywhere in the codebase implements the JWS/JWE 'crit' (critical header parameter) extension check required by RFC 7515 \u00a74.1.11 / RFC 7516 \u2014 an implementation MUST reject a JOSE object that declares a 'crit' header naming an extension it does not understand. This control is entirely absent, so jws.py/jwe.py will silently process tokens carrying unrecognized critical header extensions instead of rejecting them.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently verified by reading the full source: jose/jws.py (all 269 lines) never references 'crit' at all \u2014 sign(), verify()/_verify_signature() (lines 253-269) only check the 'alg' header and never inspect or reject on any other header field, let alone a 'crit' list naming unsupported extensions. jose/jwe.py's decrypt() (lines 60-105) contains an RFC-7516-derived comment at lines 88-92 stating the implementation \"must... process all fields... required by... the 'crit' Header Parameter\", but the code immediately following only extracts and checks 'alg'/'enc' \u2014 no crit-array read, no lookup against a supported-extensions set, no rejection path. jose/jwt.py decode() (lines 144-168) calls jws.verify() and jws.get_unverified_header() but likewise does zero crit-related validation; it only validates alg + registered claims (exp/aud/iss/etc.). A codebase-wide Cypher query for any CpgCall whose code contains 'crit' returned 0 rows, matching the analyst's own finding. So the control described by RFC 7515 \u00a74.1.11 / RFC 7516 is genuinely and completely absent \u2014 a JOSE object with e.g. {\"crit\":[\"someExt\"]} naming an extension this library doesn't implement will be processed/verified normally rather than rejected. Reachability: jws.verify and jwe.decrypt are both reachable_from_entry=true with hop_distance=0 in the graph (they are the library's public API), so any caller feeding attacker-controlled tokens into jwt.decode/jws.verify/jwe.decrypt hits this gap directly. Severity is kept LOW because this is a spec-conformance/defense-in-depth gap rather than a demonstrated direct auth-bypass: the library implements no crit-dependent extensions itself (no x5c/jku/b64-unencoded-payload handling), so there is no concrete extension today whose semantics get silently skipped to produce a signature-verification bypass; impact would materialize only if/when an application-specific or future JOSE extension relies on 'crit' being enforced."
          },
