        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-937",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Declared dependency ecdsa with version constraint \"!= 0.15\" (i.e. any other version is accepted) is the python-ecdsa package, which upstream has acknowledged is NOT resistant to the Minerva timing side-channel attack against ECDSA signing/verification (CVE-2024-23342). The version spec only excludes 0.15 and does not pin to a patched/mitigated release, so a vulnerable version can be installed. This is a components-with-known-vulnerabilities issue (CWE-937/CWE-208) independent of any request flow, consistent with jose/backends/ecdsa_backend.py using this library for signature operations.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified independently from source and graph. (1) setup.cfg:36 (`install_requires = ecdsa != 0.15 ...`) and requirements.txt:3 (`ecdsa != 0.15`) both show the declared constraint only excludes version 0.15 and does not pin a minimum/patched version \u2014 matching the graph's Dependency node exactly. (2) jose/backends/ecdsa_backend.py genuinely uses the PyPI `ecdsa` (python-ecdsa) package: it does `import ecdsa` and uses its real API (ecdsa.SigningKey/VerifyingKey, ecdsa.curves.NIST256p/384p/521p, ecdsa.util.sigencode_string) to implement sign() (lines 98-101) and verify() (lines 103-109) for ES256/384/512. (3) This backend is not a dead/test-only file \u2014 jose/backends/__init__.py:17-20 wires `ECDSAECKey` from ecdsa_backend as the fallback `ECKey` implementation whenever the optional `cryptography` package isn't importable, so the vulnerable library is on a real signing/verification code path, not merely declared. (4) CVE-2024-23342 (GHSA-wj6h-64fc-37mp, the \"Minerva\" timing side-channel) is a real, published advisory against python-ecdsa stating the library is not constant-time and the maintainers have not released \u2014 and do not plan to release \u2014 a code fix, since it would require a ground-up rewrite; they instead advise side-channel-sensitive users to switch to pyca/cryptography. Because no patched version exists, a version spec that only excludes 0.15 cannot exclude the vulnerability \u2014 every installable version remains affected. This matches CWE-937 (use of a component with a known, unresolved vulnerability), with the underlying weakness class being CWE-208 (observable timing discrepancy). Caveat: mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" when queried for this CVE, so I could not corroborate via a Metasploit-style module; Minerva-class timing attacks are academic/statistical (requiring many signature timing samples) rather than typical weaponized network exploits, so absence from that catalog is expected and not evidence against the finding."
          },
