          "ruleId": "CWE-1104",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Declared dependency docopt is pinned to ==0.6.2, the last release of a project that has been unmaintained since 2014. It is an end-of-life component with no security support; any future vulnerability in it will never be patched upstream.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently: the dependency graph confirms a single Dependency node name='docopt', version='==0.6.2' for this scan, and the real source pins it identically at requirements-dev.txt line 6 (\"docopt==0.6.2\"). Docopt's 0.6.2 release (Feb 2014) is indeed its final published release -- the upstream project has had no subsequent release since, so the \"unmaintained since 2014 / EOL / no future patches\" characterization is accurate. This supports a CWE-1104 (Use of Unmaintained Third-Party Components) classification. That said, I found no `import docopt` / `from docopt` usage anywhere in the tree (grep across the whole source tree for \"docopt\" returns only the requirements-dev.txt declaration line) -- it is a dev-only, apparently unused/transitive pin, not something exercised by the shipped library's runtime code path. exploit_search could not be checked (exploit corpus not indexed in this environment), and no specific CVE is being claimed by the analyst -- the claim is purely about maintenance status, which I verified true. Given it's dev-only and unreferenced in actual source, severity is low and this is a hygiene/SCA finding rather than an active exploitable vulnerability."
          },
